    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:1",
      "id": "thm-transitivity-of-integrality",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 states: “Since $D$ is module-finite over the nonzero ring $A$, it is nonzero.” This is false. Take $A=\\mathbb Z$ and $B=C=0$ with the unital maps; both maps are integral, while the $A$-subalgebra $D=A[b_0,\\ldots,b_{n-1}]$ inside $B$ is $0$, which is module-finite over $\\mathbb Z$. The cited dependency `thm-integrality-and-finite-module-equivalences` explicitly requires a nonzero base ring, so it cannot then be applied with base $D=0$.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "29006eac87e6d6a99fc990aeac92754862ed4fa150ae7b9c9eb785a907f97ede"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:2",
      "id": "thm-lying-over",
      "location": "1.2",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "Step 1.2 writes “$A_{\\mathfrak p}=S^{-1}A$” and invokes a maximal ideal of “$S^{-1}B$”, but $S$ is not defined until the later step 2.1: “Set $S:=A\\setminus\\mathfrak p$.” The same later step is also where nonzeroness of $S^{-1}B$ is established, despite step 1.2 already invoking it.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "599197a25351cc42dd507dae43228e3a40adc752d2d403cfaca484135d925b53"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:3",
      "id": "thm-going-down-over-normal-domains",
      "location": "1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 establishes integrality only for $A_{\\mathfrak p_1}\\subseteq S^{-1}B$. Step 1.2 then further localizes the target at $S^{-1}\\mathfrak q_1$ and treats the resulting local ring as the new $B$; step 2.1 subsequently uses “each $b_j$ is integral over $A$.” The cited `thm-integrality-commutes-with-localisation` only licenses localization by a multiplicative subset of the base, not this target-only localization. The implicit preservation claim is false: let $A=\\mathbb Z$, $B=\\mathbb Z[\\alpha]$ with $\\alpha^2-\\alpha-3=0$, and $\\mathfrak q=(3,\\alpha-1)$. Then $B$ is an integral domain integral over $A$, $\\mathfrak q\\cap A=(3)$, and $\\alpha^{-1}\\in B_{\\mathfrak q}$, but $\\alpha^{-1}$ is not integral over $\\mathbb Z_{(3)}$: its irreducible monic polynomial over $\\mathbb Q$ is $T^2+\\frac13T-\\frac13$. Thus $B_{\\mathfrak q}$ need not be integral over $A_{(3)}$.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3a861042e9e8120d6639dc6e370a0487b8e5220ec9e3de911856d763914f0ea7"
    }
